# Everything the axiomd sandbox is allowed to reach, in the form
# `flatpak info --show-permissions io.github.etf.axiomd` prints it.
#
# This file is the decision, not a record of one: the manifest's `finish-args` must
# add up to exactly these, and the installed application's own permissions must be
# exactly these — both asserted by `crates/axiomd-app/tests/packaging.rs`. Widening
# the sandbox therefore cannot happen quietly, in the manifest or by a local
# `flatpak override`.
#
# Why each one, and what is deliberately absent:
#
#   shared=network   ONLY so that pressing a remote image's placeholder can load it
#                    (D4, ratified 2026-08-02). Nothing is fetched implicitly, and
#                    the probe in packaging.rs counts requests from the far end to
#                    prove it: a document renders with zero, a press makes one.
#   shared=ipc       WebKit's own processes and X11 need it; it reaches nothing
#                    outside the sandbox.
#   sockets          wayland to draw on; fallback-x11 for a session without wayland.
#                    `x11` is listed here although the manifest asks only for
#                    wayland and fallback-x11: flatpak records fallback-x11 as the
#                    x11 flag plus the fallback flag, and decides at launch whether
#                    to bind the X11 socket at all — it does not when there is a
#                    wayland one. Observed on flatpak 1.16 by installing this
#                    manifest and reading `flatpak info --show-permissions` back;
#                    it is why this file is pinned from the installed application
#                    rather than written out from the manifest.
#   devices=dri      hardware rendering for the webview. Without it WebKit falls back
#                    to software compositing on a machine that has a GPU.
#
#   filesystems=     host:ro, and nothing else, ever. The owner ruled this on
#                    2026-08-03 (issue #23) after a document opened from Files showed
#                    broken images: under the portal alone a document arrives in a
#                    directory holding that one file, so the pictures the author kept
#                    beside it are unreachable and there is no portal that would hand
#                    them over. `:ro` is the whole of the ruling — axiomd may *read*
#                    what the reader can read, and may write nothing but the one
#                    document the portal granted it, which is still how every save
#                    goes out. `host` without the suffix, `home`, or an `xdg-`
#                    directory are each a further widening and each fails the gate.
#                    This is the one sanctioned entry here; the pin exists to catch
#                    the next one, in the manifest or by a local `flatpak override`.
[Context]
shared=network;ipc;
sockets=x11;wayland;fallback-x11;
devices=dri;
filesystems=host:ro;
